# Vulnerabilities govulncheck reports that this repo knowingly ships with.
#
# One ID per line, with the reason. Anything NOT listed here fails the
# Vulnerability scan workflow, so this file is the whole difference between a
# gate and a warning nobody reads. Adding an entry is a decision; removing one
# is what happens when a fix ships.
#
# Every entry must record: what it is, why it cannot be fixed, and what would
# let it be deleted.

# --- github.com/jackc/pgx/v4 and pgproto3/v2 -------------------------------
#
# CORRECTED 2026-10-05. An earlier version of this note said both were
# denial-of-service issues reachable only through the Postgres CDC worker,
# which prod does not run. Scanning each binary separately shows otherwise:
#
#   - cmd/management-api — the core API, in prod — reaches BOTH, because its
#     database/sql driver is github.com/jackc/pgx/v4/stdlib;
#   - cmd/postgres-consumer and cmd/postgres-producer reach both through the
#     pgx v4 pool (not built as product images, not deployed: #239);
#   - no connector does: they use lib/pq.
#
# GO-2026-5004 is SQL injection via placeholder confusion with dollar-quoted
# string literals in pgx's client-side sanitiser, not a DoS. GO-2026-4518 is a
# DoS in pgproto3 when reading server messages. Neither has a fix in the v4
# line ("Fixed in: N/A"); pgx v5 is the fix.
#
# Why they are still accepted for the moment: the sanitiser only runs in
# simple-protocol mode, which the management API does not use (database/sql,
# default extended protocol, no prefer_simple_protocol, no dollar-quoted SQL),
# and the pgproto3 issue needs a malicious Postgres server on the other end.
# That is an assessment, not a proof.
#
# Removed by migrating to pgx v5 — plans/pgx-v5.md.
GO-2026-5004
GO-2026-4518
